As Vraska falls, she thinks of division. All the pieces of herself, all the parts she doesn't want to look at.

She thinks of the captain, the leader who spent a lifetime overlooked until a dragon saw her potential.

The Golgari revolutionary who fought tirelessly for her people, no matter the cost.

The _ compleated _ queen who blinded her foes for the cause of wearing another guild's robes.

The assassin who killed too many names for her to remember or question.

And that last part, that is the one that catches her attention. What love has she shown this aspect of herself? What gratitude can she give this part? Should she thank it for all the times she _ needed _ to kill? Should she remember, presently, how difficult and different that time in her life was?

In Vraska's mind, as she passes through the portal in a haze of blue light, she sees herself, the assassin, and invites her to join this fall.

Vraska tumbles, current self with imagined past, and as she sees her destination ahead, the assassin shares her wisdom.

_ We know how to gift a kind death. _

