Meanwhile, a boy walks on a hot night under a starry sky. This place is a trap, and he is the snare.

He feels ill. Disgusted with himself. This memory is one he has forgotten many times over, sometimes by accident, often on purpose. This part of him remembers, distantly, what his adult selves know, but mostly he remembers all the parts the rest of him shut away. The boy understands. His duty is to his task. His task is withholding the ugliest knowledge of all. He is the Soldier.

Above him the sky is broad, stretching from a massive bluff on one side of the horizon to a speck of a city on the other. Its spine is a series of massive metal rings, and they buzz with the energy of a skyful of storms. Their crackle is the only sound in the air. Here, it is nighttime, and surrounding the boy are fourteen canvas tents, two sets of shoes outside of each. A section's worth of horses is tied up in the distance, the embers of the army's fires cooling themselves to sleep. He is sick of this repeating nightmare, nauseated to have to keep walking by those shoes, those horses, this silence. The boy wishes for company. No one is awake except for him—a child, a soldier, somebody's son.

He has been trapped in this night, this forgotten-then-remembered-then-forgotten darkness. It is the memory he was most glad to be rid of, the one he shoved furthest down. Alhammarret's instruction was clear that night, and even now in this memory trap, he marvels at his efficiency. He changed the course of a war this night, not that it mattered. The boy, the Soldier, stands in the middle of the tents now with clean hands despite the filth weighing down his heart.

There are footsteps. He instinctively sweeps the area with the reach of his mind. Not a soul is alive except for the horses and _ her _. His heart lifts. The captain can save him. She can rescue him from this memory. She can do anything.

The Soldier sees Vraska approaching and straightens into a salute. "Captain," he says, gesture precise. He's concerned by her response; his captain's face is sorrowful at the honorific.

_ Which Jace is this? _ he overhears.

_ The weapon _, says the child soldier to her mind, and he feels her burn with pity. He's ashamed, retreats from her mind with the feeling of it, her sympathy a sadness cloying like clotted feathers. The Soldier is the least proud version of himself—the sphinx's prodigy, the sphinx's weapon. The spy, the assassin, though Jace would never voice that. He is the Jace whose childhood was stolen and whose reality was rewritten again and again. He is the boy in possession of godlike powers. He has not learned to hate them yet.

Which is why he needs his captain.

"I need help, sir," he says in a child's small and bashful voice. He can't be older here. This is how old he was when he was a killer. This is when he was least comfortable saying what must be said now. The silence of every set of bodies in every unmoving tent, the gargantuan number scribbled on a chalkboard, the face of every friend he ever hurt on purpose … all of it weighs on the boy's shoulders. He'd cry, but he has not learned to cry yet.

Instead, the Soldier is embarrassed. He sees the captain start to tear up when he cannot. He tries to stay calm and stands at parade rest. Hands at his back. Shoulders taut. Chin up. _ I am at the ready _, he assures himself. _ I am at the ready. _

She steps closer, and in the thin light of the moon and stars he sees the captain as he always did—a superior, a moral bastion, the arbiter of right and wrong. His embarrassment deepens, for he knows he is thin, reedy, young. He does not like being near his captain in this state. His cloak is dirty with travel and nothing else. He is profoundly out of place, just barely beyond childhood and surrounded by war. The Soldier watches his commander rankle at the stillness of the campsite, the silence, the tired bags under his own eyes, days of travel on his cloak but not a knife at his side, and feels her shiver with the memory of a personal nightmare: _ He does not _ need _ a knife. _

"You don't have to call me _ sir _," Vraska says.

"You're my captain." The Soldier glances at the tents to his right and looks down. "I failed you."

She kneels to look him in the eye and the Soldier feels small, so small. He does not know what the rest of his selves know. He doesn't matter to himself yet.

"What makes you say that?"

The Soldier holds his face still. In that watercolor world of night under a full moon, the boy whispers, "I'm the reason you couldn't love all of me. I didn't want you to see me, because all I'm good at is …"

He doesn't bother finishing. The twenty-eight dead soldiers in their tents who forgot how to breathe say nothing to his truth.

He … He is being held.

The captain embraces his shoulders.

The world is washed with blue and indigo. Passing clouds form inkblots on the ground, smear slowly across the canvas tents, and in the center of the camp, the boy is held tightly by his captain.

She says to him, "You are a killer worthy of the Ochran."

"Is that all I am?"

"It is enough for me to love you."

The clouds above part. Something fundamental shifts.

He inhales as a choke but dares not let his tears fall, instead voicing the cure to the fear at the root of his being. "I'm not all bad?"

His captain smiles, as if realizing something herself. "You're not bad _ because _ you are a part of him." Vraska's voice is gentle. She does not shape her words for the ear of a child. "I don't see a heartless murderer. I see a child with a powerful gift who grows into a man who refuses the subjugation you know too intimately. You're part of him, and I love you as I love him."

"I need help," says that most isolated, most fearful part of himself, for the very first time.

The captain's embrace is warm and gentle.

He lets a tear fall and gasps. "Can you love all of me if I'm in there, too?"

She tugs him even closer.

They hold each other for a while, adult comforting child. High above, the sky is clear; clouds vanish into the beautiful blue night with a sliver of a sweet-cream moon, and the valley is awash with starlight.

The Soldier feels electric, filled with a glowing discovery. He knows who could help. He considers, flips through theorems as quick as through a times table, deduces what will be left once the plan is complete, remembers … yes. There is someone who can help in just the right way.

"I will ask for help." The boy sniffs. "Thank you, Captain." He turns to look at the moon and knows the older parts of him are picking up on his plan.

"I don't want you to die," the captain says to his back. "And I don't want you to die suffering."

The boy shakes his head firmly and glances over his shoulder at her. "There's no such thing as death," he says with certainty. "Death is a change in state."

The part of Jace that he was frightened of the most walks toward his captain, and without another word, he soundlessly disappears his body and dives into her mind.

He watches through her eyes as she walks through the empty Vrynian camp, headed for the mage-ring at the far end, and he feels Vraska think on his words.

"When I was an assassin … my work was easier when I remembered that death is a part of life."

From the inside, the Soldier feels her make a discovery, glance at a part of herself she hasn't wanted to look at for quite some time. A part he understands well.

"I know what I can do," she says aloud, and from the interior of Vraska's mind, the Soldier sees old, dark aspects of her personhood blooming like Undercity roses in shafts of sunlight.

