The Guildpact sits on his chair, at his desk, within his own prison, in the memory trap full of rubble. He is half sick of the unchanging disaster preserved outside his window like a dollhouse. The Chamber of the Guildpact still bears its immense scar, a hole gouged from stone and mortar, looking out over a sad and suffering Ravnica.

_ I was arrogant to think I could fix it. That arrogance may kill us all yet. _

When a portal appears, he assumes it is the Theorist (again) coming to force him to check his work (again), but instead he sees tendrils, a steely gaze. Thank all the ghosts in the quarter—it's Vraska. He rises from his desk and nearly trips over the rubble on the ground as he races to embrace her. He has felt what the others were up to, sensed the other aberrations' plans as if overhearing a jailbreak through a wall, and greets the facilitator of his escape with awe.

"The Theorist has Jadzi," the Guildpact warns. "I can feel it. You won't get there in time. But the others might."

Vraska nods.

"You even found the Soldier?"

"He needed saving the most."

The Guildpact looks down. "I didn't want you to see him."

"I know, but he's you." She smiles. "Are you ready?"

The rain patters and flicks across the exposed wall. It isn't the catastrophe outside that draws his attention but the mass of wood in the center of his office.

The Guildpact looks at it as if it has insulted him personally. "I quit," he says to his desk. "The Guildpact is a law created by a delusional narcissist, and it's held together by stress sweat and ten thousand years of crossed fingers. I never wanted it, I wasn't good at it, and I hope Niv-Mizzet hates being the Guildpact as much as I did. In fact, I hope he spends the rest of his long, artificial life savoring its misery."

He spits on the desk, and Vraska makes a small noise of approval.

"Good riddance." The Guildpact lets out a long, restorative breath. "Dearest?"

Vraska stares at the desk and turns it to stone. They grasp one another's hands, savoring the moment.

At last, the man who was the Living Guildpact nods with satisfaction. He flips a rude gesture at the stone desk and vanishes into the safety of his beloved's mental embrace.

