The last aberration stands on an empty beach.

The Survivor has spent his time staring at the turquoise sea, listening to the rhythm of the water, feeling the sun warm on his back.

He can feel the others, what they're doing, where they hide. He sensed the plan as they sensed it in turn. The Survivor understands where it ends. He's been concealing their machinations from the Theorist. He orchestrates the plan, urges them to tell their keepers, _ Come to the Ixalan trap. Fight your way through the doppelgängers. We'll all go through the portal to the sanctum together. _

Chandra, Ajani, Garruk (Garruk?), and Vraska will be here soon. And now it's just him, here, at the end.

He considers how to weigh his life now that there's nothing left to survive.

By his deeds? All that fills his mind is the pain he caused, the accidents he masterminded, and the loved ones he alienated. His heart sinks. He has lived valiantly, but he has prioritized the wrong things. Knowing more—to what end? Solving the unsolvable—who did he solve it for? His father's judgment still echoes in his chest. _ Selfish. Know your place. _

By his choices? He chose poorly, continuously, aggressively. He was so certain of his capability that he never stopped to consider the broader picture. He sees his friends' disappointed faces. Emmara's. Kallist's. Gideon's. Nissa's. Ral's. Vraska's …

By his crimes. He sees Kallist's face in his mind, and it still feels like his own. His friend never deserved death. Nor did the people Alhammarret and Tezzeret forced him to hurt. And Vryn … He survived Phyrexia, but he did not defeat it. He still feels what it was like to jolt a battalion into a seizure. To coax a section of soldiers to die in their sleep. Yet … none of those weighs as heavily as the crime he chose. He was ready to trade all of existence for the possibility of a future. He created the conditions for Jadzi's death. Every part of him is steeped in regret.

When he hears Vraska's steps, he turns. She's holding her shoes, barefoot on the sand, walking toward him with a sad smile.

"Will you sit with me?" is all he can ask.

She's beautiful, there on the beach. Her leggings are rolled up, as are her sleeves. Scars shine in the light underneath. She looks like she's been crying. The Survivor is embarrassed that she weeps on his account.

Vraska nods and takes a seat by his side. The tide is coming in. They sit there for a moment, watching the water.

"I'm afraid to die," he confesses. "It will not be … easy." He knows what it looks like to kill someone with a telepath's gifts. He knows his death will be both necessary and miserable.

She places a hand on his thigh and says nothing. The sun is high overhead. The sand is warm.

She smiles. "The Soldier was enthusiastic about it. 'Death is a change in state.'"

The Survivor coughs a laugh. Vraska jumps a little at the sudden sound.

"Kid's smart." He smiles and leans forward to rest his elbows on his knees.

The air smells of the sea. Time feels sunny and languid, like this moment is a symbol of forever rather than a segment of time. Maybe they'll always be here, on this beach.

He pauses. He can't escape the thought that clings to his heart like a cobweb. "The other … The Warden meant it when he told you we want you to take credit for this. I don't want the drive that got me here to be rewarded."

Vraska catches his eye. "That's true selflessness."

He looks down, ashamed. "It's not. It's just honesty."

"You are who you chose to be. Not Jace Beleren, not the Guildpact, not the creator of a Multiverse, but a man who does what is right without glory." Her eyes are golden, beaming, and her face is as open as he's ever seen it. "I've never been more proud."

The Survivor swallows. Nods. "I'm glad you'll be with me, at the end."

Vraska takes his hand. "An older version of me would say your death is just. But I … I think I just want you to feel at peace."

This isn't what the Survivor was expecting to hear. His breath is shaky. His skin feels sunburned. He feels anything _ but _ peace.

He pauses, thinks, remembers. His heart warms. He sees the sea as it is—brilliant, boundless, endlessly changing, eternally in conversation with itself. A constant metamorphosis. Even when the tide is out, even when it's turbulent and impassable, even when it's at its most destructive, it is still the sea. His skin tingles with the sensation of grand praestigial magic in his body far away, just as his scars gleam white in the sun. The sea tumbles over each edge of the horizon in front of him.

He catches her eye. "I don't want to be at peace."

"Peace is dull," she agrees.

He nods, and easy as a sigh and a sideways memory, his existence clicks into place. "I'm in progress."

Saying it aloud feels right. He feels warm, nearly feels his heart beat fast. Yes. That's who he is. That's who he's always been, even when he forgot.

Vraska is still, but he can see understanding in her face.

"I love all of you," she says at last. "Your gifts, your faults … I fell in love with a man who knew nothing about himself, and I wish I could marry the man who knew every facet of his being, shadows and all."

He curls a finger around a tendril and kisses her softly. "Our souls to the stone and returned," he recites, a Vrynian prayer of betrothal on his lips. "Encircle our union and bind our futures, pleasant and painful, spouse to spouse, marriage to death."

Their kiss deepens, and their eyes meet.

"Promise me you'll be there for Tam," he says.

The Survivor stands.

He walks to the water's edge and hears Vraska follow. He steps in. The water is inviting, its shallows warm. Little fish flit by his nine toes. The Survivor feels renewed, as if he stands on the precipice of great change.

He feels Vraska take his hand in the water. She kneels, submerged slightly, and swims a little out into the cove. He smiles and follows.

They wade close together, and he cups her face. "I'm ready for death if you'll be there with me. You're my wife in all the ways that matter."

Waves lap at the rocks in the distance.

"Jace," she says. "I want to help you." She takes a deep breath. "I know what death you deal. Your passing will be excruciating. Let me at least gift you a kind death."

The Survivor doesn't hide his shock. _ How can she offer to involve herself like this? _ Then, after a moment of thought … _ How can she not? _ "Are you sure this is what you want?"

The gorgon smiles, and it meets her eyes. She says, in the fullness of who she is, "My gift is death. It's part of me."

His relief exits as a sharp breath, and he grimaces in thanks. He was terrified of what he knew he'd have to do, remembered how excruciating death at the hands of a telepath could be.

"I will be with you to the end of your days," he says. He holds her hand tight and leans in close, forehead to forehead. "I'm every time you feel curious. I'm every time you want to understand another's perspective." His voice is thin and emotional. "I'm every time you're unafraid to start over."

The sea sighs, and the tide encourages them to shore as they embrace one final time.

