The Theorist is ready now.

His courage is held by a thin and precious chain. It hangs taut from what is left of his heart. He is going to kill the oracle. He is going to find a way to converge the Multiverses. He _ will _ succeed.

He stands on the balcony of the tower. He knows he is about to experience a harrowing psychic journey and would prefer not to pass through a wall. The sun is low with morning, the clouds blushing at the grim setup he has organized in the middle of the night. Below, the ground moves with activity, dozens of mage hunters circling the base in search of magic. Some reach claws upward; others buzz just above the surface. The Theorist wonders how well they will feast today.

Vraska, the one this Multiverse made, shakes out cloth on the tile. Her white cloak spreads wide behind her, and she squints her eyes in the morning sun. The Theorist feels … strange … to have her so close. Of all the doubles, she is the one who makes him most uncomfortable. No attraction exists between them, and yet Vraska's double stays nearby. Out of instinct? Loyalty? On the few occasions he's tried to speak with her about her past, he always leaves feeling like he's had a conversation with a construct. Vraska is not Vraska without something to fight for.

But this one has prepared a knife, and the Theorist knows that far below there will be beasts ready to dispose of the body once it is thrown over the side. He can do this. He feels ill, but his courage keeps him firm. This will work. Their math is sound.

"Bring the oracle," the Theorist commands.

This Vraska ducks inside, and the Theorist turns back to the horizon.

His world is one of sunrises. There is no darkness beyond that which he brings in, no pain for his people if they are incapable of fully feeling it. The Theorist feels brief discomfort looking upon his Multiverse. Aren't sunrises meant to be enriching? This one feels thin, like the veneer of an emotion painted on a wall. He tries to concentrate. He runs through his plan once more, feels the proof sink into his skin. But as he does, the math clicks together, the formula slides into place. Its order of operations leads to a realization, a revelation on sunrises and why this one is inadequate.

"There's no birdsong," he says aloud to no one.

Only the silent morning hears him.

His heart speeds. His hands feel clammy on the iron railing. He forgot to tell this Multiverse to create the trivial things … blackbirds singing, the sigh of wind. The Theorist realizes something the whole of him would have known instinctually—that the moments of birdsong and breeze are neither suffering nor joy. They are simply … now. This part of him never bothered with _ now _.

What makes the sunrise matter? Irritating; he cannot remember.

"Sure you have enough of you to do this?" Jadzi stands bathed in sunshine, her chin tipped in challenge.

"I am certain," the Theorist says. "I needed to purify my state and free it of indecision."

"You really are chickenshit without the rest of you. You know they talk to each other about you, right?"

He rages his horror into submission. "Show me what you see!" His eyes flash, and he enters Jadzi's mind … and is overcome.

The Theorist barges into the oracle's perception and is immediately shredded by a dust storm of possibility. It is a vast desert filled with swirling sand, and obfuscated by the storm are visions of potential, the myriad ways their moment in the sunrise could play out. The Theorist sees what he saw before—a hundred options, a thousand outcomes. But the voice he hears over the storm carries one clear fact. He needs to kill Jadzi to do it.

The knife is in his hand. He won't know how to complete the merging without this step. She's right there. But he cannot muster the courage to kill. The part of him that could do it is gone.

"I think this may be beyond my expertise."

Jadzi laughs brightly. "Oh good! It's _ this _ timeline." Without hesitation, she grabs the knife from the table in front of her.

"Wait!" yells the Theorist. But through Jadzi's perception, the Theorist sees Chandra, Ajani, and Garruk enter the sanctum and bolt toward the balcony.

Jadzi doesn't stop. Her hand moves to her throat, and an instant later—

It's too late.

The Theorist feels his telepathic link to Jadzi tug like a fish on a line and unites in her pain as she cleanly slices the knife across her throat.

He cries out in agony, and in the privacy of her mind, Jadzi appears in the storm in front of the Theorist, smiling for her final psychic words. Her throat is cut; the bright red stands out in the storm. She takes the Theorist's hand, smiling, and the Theorist knows he is doomed.

"It's all so easy for you, isn't it?" she says, her throat gleaming with still-pouring blood. "You're the part that lacks perspective." The Theorist tries to pull away, but her grip is a vise. "Keep your lids open."

