And as Jadzi's mortal body dies, the Theorist's consciousness is

Dragged

Back

To Creation

Sissoko's Law grips tight as he is launched from the balcony to the sky to the edge of the atmosphere to the edge of the vacuum of space and out into the Blind Eternities, crashing through Arcavios and the space that surrounds the plane, and all the Theorist can see is the sun hurtling backward again and again and again, ten thousand, ten million, ten billion times, until it is a solid golden hoop of chronologic impossibility.

It is a fact, he read in a book long ago, that when an oracle of Arcavios dies, they are hurled back to the creation of Arcavios, and sure as the sun screeches to a halt, he is catapulted even farther, straddling the Blind Eternities, careering backward into infinity, and he sees in front of him two planes colliding into one. Amid the chaos, formless Eldrazi horrors snake around the great ships that battle them.

This is how it happened, then. How two planes merged into one.

The Theorist sees it, feels Jadzi's consciousness evaporate and her hand release from his, feels the sensation of that hand flipping him an obscene gesture as it drifts into oblivion. And with her death, he feels a cosmic knowledge take shape.

_ I _

_ I have _

_ I have done it _

_ I have witnessed the beginning _

He says

To all of space and time

And when he looks inward

He sees himself

Or at least

The sliver of self that he can see

_ I shall be remembered for my gift to existence _

_ Suffering ends at my will _

_ All transforms at my will _

The Theorist forgets sunrises. He forgets meaning. He forgets smallness. Suffering is an imagined state. What does pain matter to a sun? To the Blind Eternities? To the immensity of the Multiverses? He is beyond body and soul and spirit. The Theorist in this moment extends into a perception beyond perception. Behold what miracles he creates from ambition alone.

He observes how the Multiverse clicks and rotates and turns as a grand machine and sees how, in all the infinite possibilities of existence, there is always a way, always a means. All he has to do is be curious enough to find it, and once he does, he will understand.

He will understand

He will

He can

He can see it all

_ No one _

_ No dragon nor dead man nor god has ever done what I have _

The being that was the Theorist that was Jace is sated

_ I know how to overwrite the Multiverse _

The Theorist returns, an infinity away, an infinity forward in time.

He lands back in his body and feels impossibly confined. The Theorist rolls out his shoulders and feels the atoms containing him. For a moment, he feels claustrophobic, not at the immensity of his sanctum and the looming net of mage-rings, but at the clumsy misery of existing in a body. He blinks away the space between his molecules and sets upon his task. "Now!" Chandra calls somewhere near his face, and his taste of enlightenment is instantly punctured by two intruders in his mind.

The aberrations. His selves. Not all of them, but enough.

They bombard his peace and charge into his body. They share his strength. Working together, they overpower him swiftly.

The Warden takes hold of his ambition, and the Theorist tries to explain that he cannot stop. He _ has _ to keep going. _ You don't understand! _

The Clean Slate dives in, attempts to wipe away the knowledge of how to merge the planes, but the Theorist won't fall so easily. He takes hold of that thread of enlightenment he felt and catapults his mind outward.

The Theorist summons a barrier of sleep behind him, a line of torpor between him and his friends. He feels them halt in place, stand behind his line.

From beyond his body, beyond the barrier, he hears Chandra cry out, "He's already starting!"

And yes, he is. He has to. Jadzi was right. He sees the fleeting image that spurred her hand to reach for the knife. There is only one way this works. He must continue the plan. The Theorist laughs through his omniscience at the madness of it all, at how there is only one way, though he cannot see exactly how, and his laugh reverberates across dimensional reality. It is a future he can only begin to fathom.

For a moment, he wonders where Vraska is. But not for one second does he stop.

The Theorist feels for the edge of this Multiverse as if it were a great blanket, plucking at its corners and feeling space-time slide through his fingers. He tugs and pulls, dragging that great fabric over the hard stone of the old Multiverse. As he links the two, vertical threads tie upward and down, connecting one to one, each to each, world to world, atom to atom.

#figure(image("008_Episode 8- Keep Your Lids Open/03.jpg", width: 100%), caption: none, supplement: none, numbering: none)

And as he weaves, the inner attack continues.

The Theorist fends off sleep, a seizure, hallucinations of Azor, visions of Vraska bleeding on a Ravnican roof.

But he persists, following his proof and connecting the two Multiverses with a thicket of cause, and as he reaches his crescendo, the Theorist is hit with a wall of arresting synesthesia. For a moment, he staggers back to consciousness and nearly vomits. Language tastes like ammonia, and his own magic screeches like an injured dog.

Another aberration crashes in from the top of his mind, summoning plague and clouding his inner landscape with thick fog and forgetfulness. The Theorist gasps, terrified, uncertain who the dead body is on the floor or how he ended up on this balcony, but with significant effort clears away the fog and remembers he was supposed to be helping. He is a helper. No one knows it but him, but he is saving the day. He reaches with omniscient power and helps one Multiverse help another.

"Fight us, coward!" Chandra, again, from beyond the barrier of sleep he raised behind him.

The Theorist speaks, but all that comes out is a string of nonsense, thanks to the aphasia cast by one of the aberrations.

This needs to end. The Theorist wrests control, scrubs off the plaque, clicks his language back into place, shoves the two aberrations as far down into his psyche as he can. But just as he is about to kick them out, he feels them remove the barrier between him and his old friends, and the other Jaces vanish from his mind.

Chandra gasps. "Mine's back!"

Garruk only growls. The heroes rush the Theorist. Without a second thought, he flicks his hand, and his friends come to a halt, standing perfectly still.

The Theorist tries to compose himself. He lets out a shuddering breath. With half a foot still planted outside of both Multiverses, he looks at his friends with omniscient eyes.

"Thank me," he says. "I did it to help all of you."

"You did it to prove it could be done!" Ajani snarls.

But all the Theorist can do is shake his tired head. "It cannot be stopped now. I must finish it."

He flicks his hand, and a mage-ring for each appears in the center of the sanctum. From the shadows emerge the cryomancer Chandra, the veil-embraced Garruk, and the warlord Ajani. Each tackles their twin into a different portal.

The Theorist shivers. He returns to the balcony and avoids looking at Jadzi on the tile, surrounded by a pool of red. He holds his hands aloft, and with the greatest telepathy the Multiverse has ever known, he weaves two Multiverses together.

But as he finishes, he shivers.

He achieved omniscience.

It was easy.

It was so easy.

